  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1136 PM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nShowers continue to push across the Mid-South ahead of a frontal\nboundary, with thunderstorm activity remaining south of the area.\nThere is still some question of if a narrow corridor of\ninstability can resurge into northeast Mississippi and the TN\nRiver overnight, although that window of opportunity continues to\nclose. Latest mesoscale analysis does show 500 J/kg of SBCAPE\njust to our south, but storms to our south have not utilized this\nthroughout the evening. If we were able to get some instability,\nthere is still ample shear present with a strengthening LLJ\nacross the region. However, current trends are not in favor of\nthis mainly stratiform precipitation developing well ahead of the\nfrontal boundary. If the atmosphere is able to somehow tap into\nany instability, damaging wind gusts and a brief tornado still\ncould not be ruled out into the overnight period. However, the\nmain concern at this point does appear to be periods of heavy\nrainfall, that could lead to some flooding in flood-prone areas.\nPWAT values are currently in the 1.6\" to 1.8\" range across the\narea, so this will be something to watch through the rest of the\novernight period.\n\nAny lingering showers and thunderstorms will exit the area by\nmidday on Wednesday, with a dry forecast building back into the\nregion for the foreseeable future thereafter. While the\naforementioned frontal boundary will aid to knock temperatures\nback down a few degrees, temperatures will rebound slightly into\nThursday with southwesterly flow. High temperatures on Thursday\nwill be a few degrees above normal, spanning the 70s across the\narea. By Thursday evening into Friday, an upper-level trough will\nswing through the region with a reinforcing frontal boundary\nreturning northwesterly flow to the Mid-South. This will allow\ntemperatures to fall to near normal to end the week and below\nnormal into the weekend. Friday's highs will remain closer to\nnormal in the 60s to low 70s, but by Saturday high temperatures\nwill fall into the 50s to low 60s. These below normal\ntemperatures will likely persist into Monday, with overnight lows\nin the 30s to 40s each night. Mid-range guidance does show a\nslight warm up near the end of the current forecast period, with\nthe CPC 6 to 10 day temperature outlook favoring above-normal\ntemperatures.",
